data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The French Revolution (1789–1799) was one of the most pivotal events","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" in human history. It was not a single event but a decade of systemic collapse and violent transformation that replaced an absolute monarchy","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" with a republic, and eventually, a military empire. The revolution was driven by a volatile mix of social inequality, economic","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" desperation, and new intellectual currents.\n\n### Causes of the Revolution\n\nThe causes of the French Revolution can be categorized into","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" social, economic, and intellectual factors.\n\n**Social Inequality: The Three Estates**\nFrance was divided into a rigid","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" social hierarchy known as the *Ancien Régime*. The population was split into three \"Estates.\" The First Estate","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" (clergy) and the Second Estate (nobility) held the vast majority of the wealth, owned most of the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" land, and were largely exempt from taxes. The Third Estate—comprising everyone from wealthy lawyers and merchants (the bourgeoisie","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":") to urban laborers and rural peasants—made up about 98% of the population. Despite carrying the entire tax","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" burden of the state, the Third Estate had virtually no political power, creating a deep sense of resentment.\n\n**Economic","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Crisis and Famine**\nBy the late 1780s, France was on the brink of bankruptcy.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Decades of lavish spending by the monarchy and expensive foreign wars—most notably France's support for the American Revolution—","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"had drained the treasury. To make matters worse, a series of bad harvests led to severe food shortages. The price of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" bread, the staple diet of the poor, skyrocketed, leading to widespread famine and \"bread riots\" in the streets of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Paris. The monarchy's inability to solve the food crisis or reform the tax system made the government appear incompetent and indifferent","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":".\n\n**The Enlightenment**\nWhile the poor were starving, the educated bourgeoisie were reading the works of Enlightenment philosophers like","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Voltaire, Rousseau, and Montesquieu. These thinkers challenged the \"divine right of kings\" and proposed that government","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" should be based on reason, merit, and a \"social contract\" between the rulers and the ruled. The idea that","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" all men possessed natural rights—liberty, property, and security—provided the intellectual framework for the revolution.\n\n**","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"Weak Leadership**\nKing Louis XVI was an indecisive leader who lacked the political will to force the nobility to pay","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" taxes. His queen, Marie Antoinette, became a symbol of royal extravagance and foreign indifference, further alienating the monarchy from","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the people.\n\n### Consequences of the Revolution\n\nThe revolution was characterized by extreme volatility, moving from the moderate goals of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a constitutional monarchy to the radical violence of the Reign of Terror, and finally to the authoritarianism of Napoleon Bonaparte. However","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", its long-term consequences reshaped the modern world.\n\n**The End of Feudalism and Absolutism","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"**\nThe most immediate consequence was the total dismantling of the *Ancien Régime*. The revolution abolished feudal dues,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" ended the privileges of the nobility and clergy, and stripped the monarchy of its absolute power. The \"Declaration of the Rights","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of Man and of the Citizen\" (1789) established the principle of legal equality, asserting that all citizens","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" were equal before the law.\n\n**The Rise of Nationalism**\nThe revolution shifted the concept of identity. People were","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" no longer \"subjects\" of a king; they became \"citizens\" of a nation. This birth of modern nationalism created","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a powerful sense of collective identity and loyalty to the state, which Napoleon later used to mobilize massive armies to conquer much of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Europe.\n\n**Legal and Administrative Reform**\nAlthough Napoleon eventually crowned himself Emperor, he preserved many of the revolution'","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"s gains through the Napoleonic Code. This civil legal system replaced a patchwork of feudal laws with a unified, written code","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" that emphasized meritocracy, property rights, and religious tolerance. This code influenced the legal systems of dozens of countries across Europe","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" and Latin America.\n\n**Global Influence**\nThe French Revolution acted as a catalyst for global change. It directly inspired","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the Haitian Revolution, the only successful slave revolt in history, which led to the independence of Haiti in 180","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"4. It also weakened the Spanish and Portuguese monarchies, fueling the independence movements in Latin America led by figures like Simón","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Bolívar.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe French Revolution was a paradox: it preached \"Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity\" while","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" executing thousands in the guillotine. Yet, despite its violence, it succeeded in breaking the back of the old aristocratic order","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". By establishing the idea that sovereignty resides in the people rather than a monarch, it laid the essential groundwork for the liberal","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" democracies of the 19th and 20th centuries.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

